          "message_id": "80da634d-6345-4829-af96-a651b664088e",
          "content": "Current structure is flat by entity type: `cache/api/{events,dates,places,peoplegroups}/`. All books share the same cache databases.\n\nPer-repository caching would make sense for a few reasons:\n\n**Pros:**\n- Clear which cache entries belong to which book — easy to clear one book without touching others\n- Can process books independently (different machines, different runs)\n- Avoids cache key collisions if two books have similar chapter names\n\n**Cons:**\n- People, places, dates are **cross-book entities** — \"Eisenhower\" extracted from BreakoutAndPursuit is the same person in CrossChannelAttack. Per-book caches would duplicate those API calls.\n- Phase 3 enrichment is entirely global (operates on `output/people/`, not per-book)\n- Deduplication is global by design\n\nA sensible split would be:\n\n```\ncache/\n├── books/\n│   ├── BreakoutAndPursuit/\n│   │   └── events/          # Event extraction (book-specific prompts)\n│   └── CrossChannelAttack/\n│       └── events/\n└── global/\n    ├── dates/                # Shared — same date across books\n    ├── places/               # Shared — same place across books\n    ├── people/               # Shared — same person across books\n    ├── peoplegroups/         # Shared\n    └── enrichment/           # Phase 3 (global)\n```\n\nEvents are book-specific (the prompt includes chapter text), so per-book makes sense. Dates, places, people are global entities that get deduplicated across books, so shared caches avoid redundant API calls.\n\nWant me to capture this as a design doc in `messages/` for future implementation?"
        }
